[ ] Move the sandbox image to a root Containerfile =sandbox-root-containerfile $podman build finds a root Containerfile with no flags, so the convention is simply the repo's default Containerfile; any repo that has one works with the sandbox. Move agents/Containerfile to the platform root and add a root .containerignore holding a single asterisk: code arrives through the mount, and without it every build ships clearhead-core/target and node_modules as context. One environment for the superproject; per-submodule images plus compose were rejected as ceremony, since scoping a run to one submodule needs only the prompt.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T15:32 #01a0dab3-2d6b-7c92-9bef-3457a5bf4c4e
[ ] Split the generic sandbox from the ClearHead driver =sandbox-driver-split $Dependency direction: the sandbox must not know ClearHead. The sandbox runner takes a repo and a command and produces a run directory, manifest, transcripts and telemetry; its manifest records exit, cost, closing message and commits, never actions. The ClearHead driver, the queue loop, prompt template and completed/blocked/unfinished classification, moves beside it and is passed in as the command. Do after sandbox-root-containerfile.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T15:32 #01a0dab3-2d95-7d53-a65e-cb88d090e1bd
[ ] Snapshot the harness into each run =sandbox-harness-snapshot $agents/ is mounted live into the running container, so editing agents/loop mid-run can corrupt a shell that reads it incrementally. agent-run copies the harness into the run directory and mounts that copy read-only: each run keeps exactly the harness it started with, which also makes runs reproducible.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T15:32 #01a0dab3-2db5-76e3-b433-81d7e9963547
[ ] Export sandbox telemetry with standard attributes =sandbox-telemetry $Sessions export OTLP to the NUC collector, which writes agent-logs, agent-metrics and agent-traces jsonl under ~/.local/state/otelcol. From a container, the collector answers on the tailscale address, not host.containers.internal. Attributes, standard only: service.name and service.version from the harness itself, host.name, deployment.environment.name sandbox or interactive, vcs.repository.name, vcs.ref.head.name, vcs.ref.head.revision, cicd.pipeline.name agent-sandbox, cicd.pipeline.run.id for the run and cicd.pipeline.task.run.id per session. No clearhead attributes: runs join to actions through commits. The driver also timestamps build and each session in the manifest. Write the convention, with this table and its reasons, to docs/telemetry.md; interactive harness configs adopt host.name and deployment.environment.name.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T15:32 #01a0dab3-2dd5-7753-9f1d-25c1a3aea18a
[ ] Query sandbox runs with duckdb =sandbox-benchmarks $A saved duckdb query file over ~/agent-runs manifests and the collector jsonl, answering: cost and time per outcome; build time against agent time; tool calls and gate runs per session; sandbox against interactive sessions. Questions first, no dashboard. Do after sandbox-telemetry.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T15:32 #01a0dab3-2df6-7862-8b3a-df005de98823
[=] Add a pi adapter and cross-vendor review =sandbox-cross-vendor-review $Harness adapters behind AGENT_HARNESS: claude and pi, each taking a prompt file and a transcript path and reporting result and cost. pi runs headless with pi -p --mode json, installs with npm install -g --ignore-scripts @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent, and takes a pinned minimal config through PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR with no extensions: pi-background-tasks would repeat the headless-wait bug. Its login lives in a dedicated agent-pi-auth volume, never a copy of the host auth.json, because OpenAI rotates refresh tokens. Review becomes a driver step run by the other vendor over the base-to-branch diff, reporting without fixing, replacing the worker's self-review. Waits on the human's one-time login inside the container.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T15:32 #01a0dab3-2e1a-79c2-a788-6901734ddadf
